WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:02.460
Instructor: Okay, so hopefully after the last section,

00:02.460 --> 00:04.230
you're now familiar with branching

00:04.230 --> 00:05.940
and the basic branch commands

00:05.940 --> 00:08.400
like git branch and git switch.

00:08.400 --> 00:09.270
We're now gonna see

00:09.270 --> 00:12.540
how we can combine changes between branches,

00:12.540 --> 00:15.360
how we can merge branches into one another.

00:15.360 --> 00:17.850
So, very important to understand branches

00:17.850 --> 00:20.370
before we can talk about merging them.

00:20.370 --> 00:22.740
This section, there's really not a whole lot

00:22.740 --> 00:24.780
of different concepts that we cover.

00:24.780 --> 00:26.460
It all has to do with merging branches

00:26.460 --> 00:28.650
and there are a few types of merges.

00:28.650 --> 00:30.930
There's one called a fast-forward merge,

00:30.930 --> 00:32.640
I'm gonna put that in critical,

00:32.640 --> 00:34.680
as well as the git merge command

00:34.680 --> 00:36.750
that I'm gonna show you in the merge commits

00:36.750 --> 00:39.480
or merge commits that are generated from git merge

00:39.480 --> 00:40.920
in some scenarios.

00:40.920 --> 00:44.190
It's really the only command I show you in this section

00:44.190 --> 00:47.220
but probably the trickiest area

00:47.220 --> 00:49.170
is something called merge conflicts

00:49.170 --> 00:50.880
and learning how to resolve them,

00:50.880 --> 00:52.170
so I've put that in yellow.

00:52.170 --> 00:55.350
It's not impossibly difficult or anything like that

00:55.350 --> 00:58.530
but there's just some new things we need to talk about

00:58.530 --> 01:00.810
when we try and merge changes together,

01:00.810 --> 01:02.490
there's conflicts, how do we resolve them?

01:02.490 --> 01:04.650
Git might get angry or might panic,

01:04.650 --> 01:07.050
so how do we fix that situation?

01:07.050 --> 01:08.310
All critical.

01:08.310 --> 01:11.490
Then, the one thing that will go in nice to have,

01:11.490 --> 01:12.870
you could totally skip this,

01:12.870 --> 01:14.850
I show how to use VS Code,

01:14.850 --> 01:16.800
the text editor that I like to use.

01:16.800 --> 01:18.360
It has some built-in functionality

01:18.360 --> 01:20.280
to help us resolve conflicts.

01:20.280 --> 01:21.480
Totally nice to have,

01:21.480 --> 01:24.180
but you would be fine never seeing that video as well.

01:24.180 --> 01:25.140
So concept-wise,

01:25.140 --> 01:27.810
there's not all that much going on in this section

01:27.810 --> 01:30.210
but the stuff that is going on is all critical.

01:30.210 --> 01:32.430
Take your time going through it, follow along,

01:32.430 --> 01:33.420
get some practice,

01:33.420 --> 01:35.910
and this is one of those last critical sections

01:35.910 --> 01:36.750
where, you know,

01:36.750 --> 01:38.160
after this, you'll have a foundation

01:38.160 --> 01:39.480
that you need to work with Git,

01:39.480 --> 01:41.340
and then everything else that we add on top

01:41.340 --> 01:43.320
will be important or nice to have,

01:43.320 --> 01:45.540
